---
// ForkKnife's front door on production, while the app itself is still being built on `forknifedev`.
//
// Condensed from docs/thesis.md, for the same reason as FortKnight's: the URL should say what the face
// argues before the tool behind it is finished. The real menu grid lives on the face branch and replaces
// this file when it merges.
import FaceLayout from "../../layouts/FaceLayout.astro";
import { facePath } from "../../lib/faces.js";

const fortknight = facePath("fortknight");
---
<FaceLayout title="ForkKnife" face="forkknife">
  <section class="panel">
    <h1>ForkKnife</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Feeding yourself is a scheduling problem wearing a recipe problem’s clothes.
      Plan the cooking, not the eating.
    </p>

    <h2>What it argues</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Nobody’s actual problem is inspiration.</strong> Recipe apps solve discovery. The question
        at six in the evening is what is already made, and what tonight costs you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leftovers are the load-bearing structure.</strong> About eight dishes cover fourteen days
        because six are eaten twice — never the next day, and at most three days later. That constraint is
        the design.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leftovers cross meals, one direction only.</strong> Sunday’s dinner is Tuesday’s breakfast;
        a morning meal’s leftovers never move to a later one. Food does not respect the slots we file it
        under.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A menu is a bill of work.</strong> Choosing a dish is choosing prep and cooking minutes on
        specific days, so the menu does not stay in the food app — its tasks land in the
        same profile <a href={fortknight}>FortKnight</a> plans from.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>That is why it is a face, not a product.</strong> Two faces, one on-device profile. A
        standalone meal planner would have to lie about what dinner costs the rest of your week.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is here now</h2>
    <p>
      The menu planner is still being built and is not on the live site yet. What works today is the
      profile it will use — and My&nbsp;Fort, which reads a fortnight exported from the Keep app.
    </p>
    <div class="button-row" id="profileActions" hidden>
      <button type="button" id="createProfileButton">Create a profile</button>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" id="profileStatus"></p>
  </section>
</FaceLayout>

<script>
  import { loadSettings, newProfileId, profileIds, saveSettings, setActiveWeights } from "../../lib/shared/user-settings.js";
  import { unusedProfileName } from "../../lib/shared/profile-names.js";
  import { PROFILE_PATH } from "../../lib/faces.js";
  import { withBase } from "../../lib/paths.js";

  // Creating a profile lives here while the questionnaires are placeholders. It writes a real, saved
  // profile rather than only making one active: `createProfile` sets the active id and leaves the saving
  // to a questionnaire, and on this branch there is no questionnaire to do it — which is the whole reason
  // the nav's "New profile…" is not offered (src/components/ProfileSwitcher.astro). The name is rolled for
  // you; you can change it on the Profile page, which is where this lands.
  const actions = document.getElementById("profileActions");
  const status = document.getElementById("profileStatus");
  const createButton = document.getElementById("createProfileButton");

  function render() {
    const saved = profileIds(loadSettings());
    actions.hidden = false;
    status.textContent = saved.length
      ? `${saved.length} profile${saved.length === 1 ? "" : "s"} saved on this device.`
      : "No profile on this device yet.";
  }

  createButton.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const settings = loadSettings(); // re-read: another tab may have saved since this page loaded
    try {
      const id = newProfileId(settings, unusedProfileName([...profileIds(settings), settings.activeWeightsId]));
      setActiveWeights(settings, { id, questionnaire: { answers: {} } });
      saveSettings(settings);
      // withBase, not the bare PROFILE_PATH: it is site-root relative, and from /forkknife/ the bare
      // form would resolve to /forkknife/profile/. FaceLayout builds the nav's link the same way.
      location.href = withBase(PROFILE_PATH);
    } catch (error) {
      status.textContent = error.message;
    }
  });

  render();
</script>
